These tests require a whole GNOME session. They run in a container, by hand and in GitLab CI, see .gitlab-ci.yml.

The tests run the real session, as Ubuntu ships it, with the packages patched here. In place of GDM, they start
gnome-session --session=ubuntu, which has the user's service manager start gnome-session's services and GNOME Shell,
headless with one virtual monitor of 1920x1080, with the Ubuntu Dock and Ubuntu's other extensions. The tests log in
and out of it, as many times as they need, and drive it through GNOME Shell: session_tests.py calls the methods of the
extension session-test@mitar.tnode.com over GNOME Shell's Eval D-Bus method, which the extension allows by turning on
GNOME Shell's unsafe mode, only ever in the test session. The extension clicks icons in the Ubuntu Dock, as the mouse
does, runs commands as the Alt+F2 dialog does, types on a virtual keyboard, moves windows and switches workspaces, and
reports the windows with their workspaces, positions, sizes and focus, the open dialogs and the clipboard.

The session has the settings which configure-gnome-desktop.sh sets with set_key, as far as their schemas are
installed: 12 static workspaces, and the Ubuntu Dock opening a new window of an application when clicked on a
workspace without one. prepare-container.sh installs this repository's session units, scripts and Chromium
configuration where configure-gnome-desktop.sh installs them. Each test runs as a new user with a service manager of
its own, so everything else, such as Firefox's profile, starts empty.

The container is to look like a machine booted with systemd as far as the session needs it: a cgroup delegated to
each user's service manager, journald, which keeps the session's log, and a system bus. There is neither logind
nor GDM, so gnome-session and GNOME Shell track the session themselves, as they do without logind, and GNOME Shell
does not create its lock screen, which the test extension then creates from GNOME Shell's own code. fake_logind.py
owns logind's name on the system bus, hands out inhibitors and announces a shutdown when a test asks it to, through an
interface logind does not have.

The tests:

  spawn-scopes    An application started at login by gnome-session, one started from the dash and a command run with
                  Alt+F2 are each in a scope of their own, together with the processes they start the moment they run,
                  none in GNOME Shell's unit, and logging out ends them all (patches/glib2.0/,
                  patches/gnome-shell/spawn-commands-in-scope.patch).
  text-editor     Text Editor started from the dock, a file opened from a terminal and a launch from the dock on a
                  workspace without its window each open a new window on the current workspace, focused. Logging out
                  lists Text Editor in GNOME Shell's dialog, as having unsaved work. After logging in again, the
                  windows return to their workspaces without being focused and without switching the workspace, the
                  launch opens a new window where the user is, and the document never saved has the text typed into it.
  ptyxis          The same for Ptyxis, whose windows return also to their positions and sizes, each with the screen it
                  showed and a new shell in the directory of the old one, with Ubuntu's .bashrc. Logging out while a
                  program runs in it lists it, as Terminal, in GNOME Shell's dialog.
  firefox         The same for Firefox, with links opened from a terminal with xdg-open, each opening in a new window
                  on the current workspace, and each window returning with its page. Logging out has to end Firefox
                  cleanly, with its session saved, before GNOME Shell shuts down, which needs the patches of
                  patches/xdg-desktop-portal-gtk/ and quit-at-session-end.patch of patches/firefox/. Otherwise the
                  next start recovers from a crash, and the window opened for the launch after logging in replaces
                  the first restored one. A link opened after the restore still lands on the workspace of a restored
                  window: Firefox marks every restored window as demanding attention, and Desktop Icons NG activates
                  such a window when the workspace changes to one without windows.
  chromium        Chromium's helpers are in the scope its launch put it into, while the browser process moves into a
                  scope of its own. Logging out ends it without a crash and with the exit type SessionEnded, and its
                  windows return to their workspaces, positions and sizes.
  chromium-quit   Ctrl+Q quits Chromium through this repository's extension and native messaging host as a logout does,
                  and the next start restores its windows to their workspaces.
  shutdown        stop-application-scopes-before-shutdown.service holds a delay inhibitor, and when logind announces
                  a shutdown, Chromium's browser process exits before the inhibitor is released, without a crash.
  lock-screen     Without reserve-space@mitar.tnode.com, locking the screen resizes a maximized window, as GNOME Shell
                  disables the dock. With the extension enabled at login, as configure-gnome-desktop.sh enables it, the
                  window keeps its size through locking and unlocking. The locked screen offers no screenshots
                  (patches/gnome-shell/no-screenshots-while-locked.patch).

After every test, the script checks that no unit of the user's service manager failed, as at-spi-dbus-bus.service
does at a logout without systemd/user/at-spi-dbus-bus.service.d/.

For the tests, prepare-container.sh also configures the browsers differently from a fresh profile: Firefox restores
the last session at startup and opens links from other applications in a new window, as the user's profile does, and
saves its session every second, through /etc/firefox/policies/policies.json. Chromium starts without the questions of a
first start, and continues where it left off, which is not its default, through /etc/chromium.d/session-test and a
policy. The tests serve their pages from a web server on localhost, and set Firefox as the default browser.

prepare-container.sh installs the session and the patched packages, so it needs the .deb files built here, and
Chromium from the xtradeb repository in sources.list.d/. run-session-tests.sh then runs the tests. Both run as root in
a privileged container, and refuse to run anywhere else. With rootless Docker, from the root of the repository, and
the packages of the local repository:

  docker run --rm --privileged -v "$PWD:/repo:ro" -v /usr/local/lib/debs:/debs:ro -v /tmp/session-test:/out \
    registry.gitlab.com/tozd/docker/base:ubuntu-resolute bash -c '
      apt-get update -q && apt-get install -y -q ca-certificates &&
      cp /repo/sources.list.d/xtradeb-ubuntu-apps-resolute.sources /etc/apt/sources.list.d/ &&
      /repo/tests/session/prepare-container.sh /debs &&
      /repo/tests/session/run-session-tests.sh /out/run spawn-scopes text-editor ptyxis firefox chromium \
        chromium-quit shutdown lock-screen'

A later DEBS_DIR replaces packages of the same name of an earlier one, so a build to test can follow the local
repository. prepare-container.sh can run again on a prepared container, for example without DEBS_DIR to install a
changed file of this repository. It then keeps every installed package as it is, as apt-get would otherwise replace
a package built here with Ubuntu's package of the same version. The tests need a container prepared only once, so a
container saved after prepare-container.sh can run them again and again.

The driver runs outside of the user's service manager, as a login session does. From there, systemd-run --scope
cannot move a process into a scope, because the user's service manager has no permission to move it. A test
therefore runs a command which a terminal would run in a service of the user's service manager instead, with
KillMode=process, so that the processes the command starts keep running when it exits, as in a terminal.

Each check prints a line starting with PASS or FAIL, and the script exits with 0 only when all passed. OUT_DIR
receives a directory for each test, with result.json, the journal written meanwhile, the output of gnome-session's
leader at each login, and the settings of configure-gnome-desktop.sh which could not be set, and logind.json, with
the inhibitors fake_logind.py handed out and the shutdowns it announced. A test given more than once reports each
further run in a directory whose name ends with the run's number.

The container differs from a desktop: the session renders in software without a GPU, input comes from a virtual
keyboard and not from a person, and a lock screen without GDM unlocks without asking for the password.
